@startuml seq-key-to-frame
'' PlantUML over Mermaid: the point is WHEN each step runs relative to the frame boundary, and that an
'' ordinary posted task (re-frame's queue) runs AFTER the frame Chromium renders right after a key. A sequence
'' diagram with one phase per build states that ordering exactly. Purple = keyboard input, blue = re-frame's
'' queue, teal = the renderer (the slides engine, and the frame it renders).
!include _vv-theme.iuml

title A → key on a slide show: why it showed a frame late, and the fix (docs/scientific/12 §8)

participant "keyboard\n(a discrete input event)" as K KEYMAP_TINT
participant "keydown handler\n(key-scroll! · keymap resolver)" as H KEYMAP_TINT
participant "re-frame queue\n(a posted next-tick task)" as Q REFRAME_TINT
participant "slides engine\n(renderer.pdf)" as E RENDERER_TINT
participant "frame\n(the rendering update)" as F RENDERER_TINT

== baseline (A): two queued hops ==
K -> H : keydown, dispatched at <latex>t_0</latex>
activate H
H -> Q : rf/dispatch [:nav/scroll {:dx :right}]
deactivate H
note over Q #E2EBF3: posted during an input event:\nChromium renders the next frame FIRST
F -> F : frame N — still the old slide
Q -> Q : :nav/scroll → :dom/scroll → scroll delegate\n→ rf/dispatch [:slides/command :next]  (queued again)
Q -> E : :slides/command → command!
activate E
E -> E : scrollTop := the next slot  (just after frame N)
deactivate E
F -> F : frame N+1 shows the slide
note right of F #E3F2ED: median <latex>t_{\mathrm{pre}} - t_0 \approx 24\,\mathrm{ms}</latex>\n(about a frame and a half)

== intervention (C): the view move runs inside the keydown handler ==
K -> H : keydown, dispatched at <latex>t_0</latex>
activate H
H -> E : commands/dispatch-now! → rf/dispatch-sync [:nav/scroll]\n→ :dom/scroll → delegate → step! → command!
activate E
E -> E : scrollTop := the next slot  (<latex>\approx 0.4\,\mathrm{ms}</latex> after <latex>t_0</latex>)
deactivate E
deactivate H
F -> F : frame N shows the slide
note right of F #E3F2ED: median <latex>t_{\mathrm{pre}} - t_0 \approx 8\,\mathrm{ms}</latex>\n(half a frame on average)
note over Q #E2EBF3: a blanked screen or a half-typed slide number\nstill goes through :slides/command (step!)
@enduml
